You know, I think I've changed my mind.

The thought of fooproof armor being only one increment farther from
dust than normal armor initially seemed pretty scary.  But the key
word there is 'initially' as now I think it must create an edgier and
less certain feel for the post-castle game, which translates to
greater excitement and interest.  And this mainly only affects
genoless anyway, right?  It will add some serious extra teeth to that
conduct, which I think is good.  (For example, I went genoless in a
recent vanilla ascension not out of some conscious decision to do so,
but more out of pure accident or laziness or indifference.  In Spork,
the decision to go genoless should now carry a lot more weight.)

One could even argue that the Spork take on this armor-destruction
behavior is more logical/coherent than the vanilla handling; that is,
your fooproof armor is only "rustproof" or "fireproof" or whatever...
not *destruction*-proof.  And in my imagination, your typical arch-
lich is One Nasty Dude whose spell of destroy armor is not going to be
thwarted by your measly scroll-based attempts at erode-proofing.
Which, while I'm on this "coherence" tack, leads me to...

YANI: New spell for high-level spellcasters which could enchant armor
such that it *is* able to withstand a Destroy Armor hit.  I know this
doesn't help the whole problem of the uber powerful late-game wizard,
but it does seem that a character with great magical skill could
protect or "fix" armor against magical damage more effectively than a
less adept valk/tourist/whatever who is foo-proofing simply by reading
scrolls.

> Which changes do you consider disproportionate and excessive, and why?

Used to be mainly those mentioned above, but not so much now.  (Except
I still don't like Touch of Death being removed from the instadeath-if-
unprotected category.  I mean...it's the touch of *death*, right?
However I do like your treatment of death rays hitting PCs with
reflection.)  In any case, all of this, I'm sure, will come across
very differently in actual play than on your changelog.  Which means I
need to stop trying to beef up my NAO stats and spend more time in the
Spongeon...



